---
/**
 * Reader education, question-first. The glossary defines terms; this page answers the
 * questions a first-time visitor actually arrives with. Prose stays in this file per the
 * strings-module policy (sentences interleave links and emphasis).
 */
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import { SITE } from '../constants/site';
import { STRINGS } from '../constants/strings';
import { formatDate } from '../data/format';
import { atlas } from '../data/load';
import * as route from '../data/routes';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const { manifest } = await atlas();
---

<Base title={STRINGS.faqPage.title} description={STRINGS.faqPage.description} section="faq">
  <div class="prose-shell">
    <PageHeader
      eyebrow={STRINGS.faqPage.eyebrow}
      title={STRINGS.faqPage.heading}
      standfirst={STRINGS.faqPage.standfirst}
    />

    <div class="prose-page">
      <h2 id="what-is-the-cfr">What is the CFR?</h2>
      <p>
        The Code of Federal Regulations is the official rulebook of the US federal government.
        When Congress passes a law, agencies write the detailed rules that put it into practice
        — what a food label must say, how much of a pollutant a plant may emit, how a pension
        must be reported. Those rules, collected and organised into 50 subject-matter titles,
        are the CFR. It is the day-to-day law that businesses and agencies actually operate
        under.
      </p>

      <h2 id="what-is-the-ecfr">What is the eCFR, and how is it different?</h2>
      <p>
        The <a href="https://www.ecfr.gov" rel="external noopener">eCFR</a> is the government's
        continuously updated online edition of the CFR — new rule changes appear within days.
        The printed CFR is republished only once a year, so the eCFR is what anyone who needs
        current rules actually reads. This site measures the eCFR.
      </p>

      <h2 id="is-this-official">Is this site an official government source?</h2>
      <p>
        No. {SITE.name} is an independent, open-source project with no government affiliation.
        Every page links back to the official text on the eCFR, and every number here is this
        project's own measurement. For legal purposes, only the annual printed CFR and the
        Federal Register are authoritative — details on the
        <a href={route.about()}>about page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="how-current">How current is the data?</h2>
      <p>
        {
          manifest.source_date
            ? `The current build reflects the eCFR as of ${formatDate(manifest.source_date)}.`
            : 'The source date for this build is recorded in the footer.'
        }
        The site re-measures the eCFR every weekday, and every page footer carries the exact
        date its data reflects. A change the government published yesterday is typically
        measured here within a business day.
      </p>

      <h2 id="why-word-counts">Why measure regulation in words?</h2>
      <p>
        Words are a blunt instrument — but an honest one. Counting pages depends on formatting;
        counting “rules” depends on what you call a rule; words can be measured the same way for
        every agency and compared over time. A word count will not tell you whether a rule is
        wise, but it tells you where the bulk of the rulebook sits, which is where research
        starts.
      </p>

      <h2 id="why-two-numbers">Why do agencies show two different numbers?</h2>
      <p>
        Because some rules have more than one agency in charge. An agency's
        <strong>total regulation</strong> counts every rule it manages in full — shared or not —
        which is the right way to state its responsibility. Its
        <strong>share of all regulation</strong> splits jointly managed rules evenly between
        their agencies, which is the right way to compare or add agencies without counting any
        rule twice. When an agency shares nothing, the two numbers are identical. The
        <a href={route.glossary()}>glossary</a> has the full definitions.
      </p>

      <h2 id="what-is-not-measured">Why do some values say “Not measured”?</h2>
      <p>
        When something could not be measured — a fetch failed, a document would not parse — this
        site says so instead of guessing. A dash with a reason can be investigated; a silently
        wrong number cannot. The <a href={route.dataQuality()}>data quality page</a> lists every
        unmeasured value in the current build, with its reason.
      </p>

      <h2 id="how-words-counted">How exactly are words counted?</h2>
      <p>
        By parsing the eCFR's official XML and counting the words of regulation text, with
        headings, authority notes, source citations and tables of contents excluded. Nothing is
        estimated from file sizes or extrapolated. The precise rules — written to be disagreed
        with — are on the <a href={route.methodology()}>methodology page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="can-i-use-the-data">Can I use this data?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes. The project is open source, and everything on the site is available as JSON from
        the <a href={route.apiPage()}>public API</a> under the same measurement rules — anonymous
        access works immediately, and a free key raises the rate limit.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
